# PART C — GLOBAL DATA MODEL AND API CONTRACT RULES

## C1. CANONICAL IDENTIFIERS

Use stable identifiers consistent with the existing repository.

Prefer opaque IDs.

Do not expose internal database document paths as public identifiers unless already established as an intentional API contract.

Every domain entity must have:

- Stable identifier.
- Creation timestamp.
- Update timestamp where mutable.
- Version or concurrency token where appropriate.
- Owner reference.
- Scope reference where applicable.

## C2. VERSIONING

Mutable records must use optimistic concurrency where supported.

An update must either:

- Succeed against the expected version.
- Return a conflict.
- Return an explicit stale-version error.

Never silently overwrite a newer record.

## C3. PAGINATION

Collection endpoints must use bounded pagination.

Default page size:

25.

Maximum page size:

100.

Use cursor pagination where the provider supports it.

Use deterministic ordering.

Never return unbounded collections.

## C4. API RESPONSE FORMAT

Preserve the existing API response convention.

If no established convention exists, use:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {},
  "meta": {
    "requestId": "request-id",
    "correlationId": "correlation-id",
    "timestamp": "ISO-8601"
  }
}

For errors:

{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "code": "STABLE_ERROR_CODE",
    "category": "VALIDATION",
    "message": "Human-readable message",
    "retryable": false,
    "details": []
  },
  "meta": {
    "requestId": "request-id",
    "correlationId": "correlation-id",
